      },
      "worked": "For $z=1$, $z!=1$, but $x!+y!\\ge2$, so there is no solution.\n\nFor $z=2$, $z!=2$. Both positive factorials must equal $1$, giving $x=y=1$. Indeed, $1!+1!=2!$.\n\nSuppose $z>2$. Neither $x$ nor $y$ can be at least $z$, because its factorial alone would be at least $z!$. Hence $x,y\\le z-1$, and\n\n$$x!+y!\\le2(z-1)!<z(z-1)!=z!.$$\n\nThis contradicts the equation. Therefore the only solution is $(x,y,z)=(1,1,2)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
